{"name":"Club Moss","hex":"#6b977a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"club moss","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various simple microphyllous vascular plants, of the family Lycopodiaceae, that resemble mosses and produce spores."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/club_moss"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lycopodiopsida","blocks":[{"text":"Lycopodiopsida is a class of vascular plants also known as lycopsids, lycopods, or lycophytes. Members of the class are also called clubmosses, firmosses, spikemosses and quillworts. They have dichotomously branching stems bearing simple leaves called microphylls and reproduce by means of spores borne in sporangia on the sides of the stems at the bases of the leaves. Although living species are small, during the Carboniferous, extinct tree-like forms (Lepidodendrales) formed huge forests that dominated the landscape and contributed to coal deposits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Lycopodium_plant.jpg/330px-Lycopodium_plant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lycopodiopsida","wikidata":"Q1149748"},{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss","wikidata":"Q25347"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lepidodendron.png/500px-Lepidodendron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lepidodendron.png/1280px-Lepidodendron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lepidodendron.png/500px-Lepidodendron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Artist's impression of a Lepidodendron","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALepidodendron.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/LepidodendronOhio.jpg/500px-LepidodendronOhio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/LepidodendronOhio.jpg/1280px-LepidodendronOhio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/LepidodendronOhio.jpg/500px-LepidodendronOhio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"External impression of Lepidodendron from the Upper Carboniferous of Ohio","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALepidodendronOhio.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Lycopod_axis.jpg/500px-Lycopod_axis.jpg